The issues raised by some of the works mentioned above can be roughly described as follows:The class of environments for the CAB1 algorithm of  is formed by environments that are
{{formula:ba5f859f-8b79-49b8-ae15-84f3864b56c4}} –uniformly locally Lipschitz and the strategy CAB1 needs to know {{formula:92e42184-c960-4136-8f2f-0259ce2bafe4}} 
to get the optimal dependency in the number {{formula:f134b1ac-6d80-48a7-9f99-915a5a2e2579}} of arms pulled;

For the Zooming algorithm of , it is formed by environments that are 1–Lipschitz with respect to a fixed
and known metric {{formula:6c98b6b6-b69a-4cde-ad82-2d8b95072610}} ;

The HOO algorithm of  basically needs to know the pseudo-metric {{formula:ea878d86-47da-46b5-b1db-671afa73c941}} 
with respect to which {{formula:2e176eaa-be30-4ba3-a6b5-8822c78968e3}} is weakly Lipschitz continuous, with Lipschitz constant
equal to 1;

Other examples include the UCB-air algorithm (which relies on a smoothness parameter {{formula:99fe32af-c4a7-4641-87b4-9e271741097b}} , see ),
the OLOP algorithm (smoothness parameter {{formula:9fc8726d-76ce-47fa-a115-ed94d0178bad}} , see ), the LSE algorithm (smoothness parameter {{formula:f0bdf2be-c165-4cf6-b52b-c4c2e25327ac}} , see ),
the algorithm presented in  and so on.Adaptation to the unknown smoothness is needed.
